>
  <article class="post-page">
    <img src="/ercot-queue-by-county.webp" alt="A glowing circuit-board map of Texas dotted with energy nodes floating over the Dallas skyline at sunset, with solar panels, battery storage containers, a gas plant and transmission towers below" class="post-image" width="1248" height="832" />

    <div class="post-content">
      <h1>Lining Up for the Grid: Where 438 GW of New Texas Power Wants to Plug In</h1>
      <p class="post-card-date"><time datetime="2026-10">October 2026</time></p>

      <h2>📘 Summary</h2>
      <p>Every power plant that wants to connect to the Texas grid has to get in line first. That line is ERCOT's generation interconnection queue, and ERCOT publishes it every month in its GIS Report. The September 2026 report lists <strong>1,758 proposed projects totaling 438.5 gigawatts</strong>, spread across <strong>192 of Texas's 254 counties</strong>.</p>
      <p>To put that in perspective, it's roughly five times the most power Texans have ever used at one moment. It's also close in size to the other side of the boom: more than 474 GW of large-load requests, mostly data centers, waiting to <em>draw</em> power from the same grid, as we covered in <a href="/posts/the-20-percent-walkaway/">The 20% Walkaway</a>. Both queues are full of projects that will never be built. But together they show where developers are betting, and which communities may be next to host a solar farm, a battery yard or a gas plant.</p>
      <p>We added up the September queue county by county. Here's what stands out:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Pecos County is in a league of its own.</strong> 56 projects and 21.4 GW, nearly double the next county and about 5% of the entire statewide queue.</li>
        <li><strong>Batteries and solar dominate.</strong> Battery storage makes up about 36% of queued capacity (157 GW) and solar about 34% (150 GW). Gas is 18% (79 GW) and wind 11% (48 GW).</li>
        <li><strong>Gas is concentrated.</strong> In Wilbarger, Reeves, Hill and Fisher counties, gas is the largest fuel in the queue, including a single 3,000 MW gas project in Fisher County.</li>
        <li><strong>Most of it is early.</strong> About two-thirds of the queued capacity (290 GW) doesn't have a signed interconnection agreement yet. Only about 10 GW is listed as under construction.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>🗺️ The Top 15 Counties</h2>
      <p>These 15 counties hold 407 projects and 127.7 GW, about 29% of all the capacity in ERCOT's queue. Capacity is the total nameplate size of every proposed project in the county; "largest fuel" is the fuel type with the most megawatts there.</p>
