          <tr><td>11</td><td>San Patricio</td><td>26</td><td>6.65 GW</td><td>Battery</td></tr>
          <tr><td>12</td><td>Deaf Smith</td><td>11</td><td>6.39 GW</td><td>Solar</td></tr>
          <tr><td>13</td><td>Fisher</td><td>13</td><td>6.38 GW</td><td>Gas</td></tr>
          <tr><td>14</td><td>Zapata</td><td>27</td><td>6.29 GW</td><td>Solar</td></tr>
          <tr><td>15</td><td>Lamar</td><td>24</td><td>6.05 GW</td><td>Solar</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h3>What's behind the numbers</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Pecos County</strong> has a bit of everything: about 8.9 GW of solar, 5.9 GW of gas, 4.7 GW of wind and 1.9 GW of batteries. Its largest single entry is the 1,500 MW Big Canyon Wind project. Amazon's off-grid gas plant, which we covered in <a href="/posts/the-biggest-polluter/">The Biggest Polluter</a>, isn't meant to connect to the ERCOT grid, so it isn't part of these numbers.</li>
        <li><strong>Brazoria County</strong>, south of Houston, is the battery capital of the queue, with about 5.7 GW of storage proposed, plus solar, wind and gas.</li>
        <li><strong>Harris County</strong> is almost all batteries: about 6.2 GW of its 6.8 GW is storage, sited close to the region's biggest demand center.</li>
        <li><strong>Wilbarger, Reeves, Hill and Fisher counties</strong> lead with gas. Fisher County's 6.4 GW includes a single 3,000 MW gas project, Brazos Energy Security, and Hill County's largest entry is a 1,400 MW gas plant.</li>
        <li><strong>Lamar County</strong>, on the Red River, includes a 1,232 MW pumped-storage hydro project named Southeast Oklahoma Pumped Storage, which ERCOT lists under Lamar County.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>⚠️ What the Queue Does and Doesn't Tell You</h2>
      <p>A spot in the queue is a request, not a promise. Developers often file for more sites than they'll ever build, and many projects drop out after the first studies show what grid upgrades would cost. In the September report, about 66% of the queued capacity hadn't reached a signed interconnection agreement, and only about 2% was listed as under construction.</p>
      <p>ERCOT also doesn't publish exact project locations in this report, only the county. So the table shows where developers are looking, not which property line a project will sit on. If a large project is listed in your county, the next places to look are your county commissioners' agendas, any tax abatement requests, and TCEQ air permits for gas plants. Our <a href="/guides/data-center-near-you/">Data Center Near You guide</a> walks through how to find them.</p>

      <div class="queue-download" id="download">
        <h2>📥 Get the Full County Spreadsheet</h2>
        <p>The top 15 is only the start. Our spreadsheet lists <strong>all 192 counties</strong> with a project in ERCOT's queue, with the number of projects, total MW and GW, and the largest fuel type for each. It's sortable and filterable, so you can find your county in seconds.</p>
        <p><strong>It's free when you sign up for the Power Grab TX Morning Briefing</strong>, our weekday summary of the Texas data center and grid news that matters, from ERCOT and PUC decisions to local votes and permit hearings.</p>
        <form action="https://forms-worker.marc-897.workers.dev/newsletter" method="POST" class="queue-signup-form">
